# BSan gate of libavutil/formats wave 2 at ac34a06476 — FAILED (lib, equiv, ub_safe)

Gate: clippy, plain, asan-ubsan, tsan pass. `bsan` lib (1 failed of 539), equiv and ub_safe abort on
"access an allocation that has been freed" inside C's av_channel_layout_from_string:
channel_layout.c:342 `(*endptr && *endptr != '+')` and channel_layout.c:417 `!*end`.
bsan ub_unsafe passes (72).

Orchestrator triage:
- deterministic: lib's isolated child test
  libavutil::channel_layout::unit_tests::channel_layout_allocation_failures_leave_valid_layouts
  fails 3/3 alone;
- reproduced WITHOUT Rust: a pure-C program built with the bsan clang plugin + runtime (the bsan
  variant's own flags) calling av_channel_layout_from_string(&l, "stereo") on a string literal
  reports the identical error at channel_layout.c:342. Source and command:
    /tmp/bsan-c-probe/probe.c (copied below); clang -fpass-plugin=libbsan_plugin.so ... -lavutil (bsan build)
- `endptr`/`end` are written by uninstrumented glibc strtol/strtoull, the same class as formats
  review wave 0's av_strtod artifact (eval.c:120, endptr from strtod), which was timing-dependent there.
Verdict candidate: BorrowSanitizer false positive on C reads through glibc-written end pointers,
independent of the wrappers. Promotion is blocked until the reviewed wave passes the BSan gate.

## C-only probe
#include <stdio.h>
#include <libavutil/channel_layout.h>
int main(void) {
    const char *in[] = {"stereo", "FL+FR", "3c", "0x3", "ambisonic 1", "5.1(side)"};
    for (int i = 0; i < 6; i++) {
        AVChannelLayout l = {0};
        int r = av_channel_layout_from_string(&l, in[i]);
        printf("%s -> %d (%d ch)\n", in[i], r, l.nb_channels);
        av_channel_layout_uninit(&l);
    }
    return 0;
}

